ck.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Distribution-Catalogue-480x270.pngzCover Image Preview  aeLoaderRecord xls4ffi081bba71-3fd5-46a2-9647-aebf5726dd18s7655b7d6-df3d-4249-be9a-5f4b89fa8dd508292nam a2201153ui in0000030223120250403134842.0a||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu220122s2022||||xx ||||go|||||||| u|eng|d  als93435  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW00aPostcards from the Anthropocene 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bdpr-barcelona,c2022  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"This book includes various responses to the geopolitical conditions of our tangent times through collections of visual materials and theoretical explorations with critical positionings. The book expands on the Anthropocene theory by exploring its relations with the aesthetic concerns in contemporary representations through their geopolitical ramifications. We conceptualize postcards as documentary space-time snapshots, which convey complex assemblages of dynamic, non-linear, unpredictable, ad-hoc networks between interdependent and transcalar actors in deep time. The postcards we assemble raise questions about the ethical and political challenges of the dominant modes of technoscientific knowledge production, modes that are constituted through existing power relationships, subject positions, and differences, and that perpetuate current inequalities. They catalyse speculative and creative geopolitical imaginaries and collective subjectivities that recalibrate existing value systems and indicate alternatives. This edited publishing project curates and presents submissions from significant scholars and practitioners across multiple disciplines in arts and humanities. In the emerging field of the Anthropocene aesthetics, the contributors investigate the possibilities of the postcard unfolding across diverse temporalities and enable complex associations between the present and the futures it constructs, both imagined and unimaginable. The transdisciplinary nature of the book makes it a key publication in the field of geohumanities. Its publication marks an exciting event for all areas of research concerned with the Anthropocene debate - Anthropocene studies, environmental humanities, political ecology, nuclear humanities, and climate change studies."--cprovided by distributor  aCC BY-NC-ND10aArchitectural criticism.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500658310aClimatic changes.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8502703710aCloud computing.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh200800488310aEarth sciences.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8504046810aGeopolitics.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8505419210aIndustrial design.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8503719910aNatural resources.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8509025410aPhotography, Artistic.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8510126917aArchitectural criticism.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008131790http://id.worldcat.org/fast/81317917aClimatic changes.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008642290http://id.worldcat.org/fast/86422917aCloud computing.2fast0(OCoLC)fst017458990http://id.worldcat.org/fast/174589917aGeopolitics.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009410450http://id.worldcat.org/fast/94104517aGeoscience.2fast0(OCoLC)fst0090072917aIndustrial design.2fast0(OCoLC)fst017301900http://id.worldcat.org/fast/173019017aNatural resources.2fast0(OCoLC)fst010344030http://id.worldcat.org/fast/103440317aPhotography.2fast0(OCoLC)fst010619640http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1061714 7aDiscursive works.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026089 7aEssay Collection.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014240321 aArmstrong, Leila,econtributor.1 aAtherton, Jo,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb20220186581 aBarla, Josef,econtributor.0http://i